<!DOCTYPE html>
{#
  ============================================================================
  HYPER · Root layout
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Order matters here:
    1. Fonts (preconnect early)      2. {% seo %} + {% qumra_head %}
    3. style.css (the design system) 4. :root overrides from Theme Editor
    5. Tailwind CDN + config mapped onto the CSS vars
    6. Swiper / GLightbox CSS + JS
  Body:
    header template → {% content %} → footer template → global overlays
    → __QUMRA_CONFIG__ → {% qumra_scripts %} → main.js → Alpine
  NOTE: {% qumra_head %} / {% qumra_scripts %} do NOT ship Tailwind, fonts,
        Alpine, Swiper or GLightbox — they are all loaded manually below.
  ============================================================================
#}
{% set lang = localization.language | default('ar', true) %}
{% set isRtl = lang != 'en' %}

{# ---- Fonts: display + body per script, plus the numerals face ---- #}
{% set fontDisplayAr = settings.fontDisplayArabic | default('Amiri', true) %}
{% set fontDisplayEn = settings.fontDisplayLatin  | default('Cormorant Garamond', true) %}
{% set fontBodyAr    = settings.fontBodyArabic    | default('IBM Plex Sans Arabic', true) %}
{% set fontBodyEn    = settings.fontBodyLatin     | default('Manrope', true) %}
{# Numerals ride on the Latin BODY face, not the display one. The display face
   is a high-contrast serif — its figures are old-style and beautiful in a
   sentence, and unreadable in a price column. #}
{% set fontNum = fontBodyEn %}

{# ---- Shape, elevation and rhythm ----
   These were five Theme Editor knobs (cornerStyle, buttonStyle, cardShadow,
   sectionSpacing, containerWidth). The global settings are now COLOURS AND
   FONTS ONLY, so the values are fixed here to the ones the design was drawn
   for: square corners, hairlines instead of shadows, and a generous vertical
   rhythm. Every section that needs a different radius or a tighter band
   already carries its own setting, which is where that choice belongs — a
   global "rounded corners" switch could never know that the product card, the
   payment badge and the subscribe pill want different answers. #}
{# HADIYA · Luxe: near-square editorial corners, sharp buttons — jewellery-box, not toy-box. #}
{% set rCard = '4px' %}{% set rInput = '2px' %}{% set rMedia = '2px' %}
{% set rBtn = '2px' %}
{% set secPy = 'clamp(40px, 4.5vw, 64px)' %}{% set secPySm = '40px' %}
{# 1264 + the 32px of padding `.hy-container` adds on each side = a 1200px
   MEASURE, which is what this design is drawn to — the floating header card is
   1200 wide and the hero's copy starts 120px in on a 1440 viewport. Changing
   this without changing `--container-px` changes the measure with it. #}
{% set containerWidth = '1264' %}

{# ---- Colour override switch ----
   Off, the whole colour block below is simply not printed and every token
   falls back to the palette already declared in assets/style.css. That is the
   revert: nothing is reset or cleared, the merchant's picked colours stay in
   the settings and come back the moment the switch goes on again.

   Same mechanism as `colors_enabled` on each section — the switch IS the
   revert. It covers colours only; fonts, shape, elevation and rhythm are
   printed unconditionally further down, because those have no equivalent
   "original" to fall back to beyond their own defaults. #}
{% set customColors = settings.useCustomColors | default(true) %}
{# Off = the theme's OWN palette (the defaults below), not the Core's in
   style.css: a dark theme fell back to the Core's light paper under its own
   dark-ground rules. `cs` is the object the colour lines read from. #}
{% set cs = settings if customColors else {} %}

<html lang="{{ lang }}" dir="{{ 'rtl' if isRtl else 'ltr' }}"{% if settings.enableAnimations == false %} class="hy-no-motion"{% endif %}>
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
    {# ===== Editor-only furniture =====
       Empty sections draw a grey skeleton and an «أضف…» hint so the merchant
       can see what to fill in the builder. Customers must never see that: on
       the live store (the top window — the builder previews in an iframe, the
       same test the scroll reveal in main.js uses) every `[data-hy-empty]`
       section and `.hy-editor-only` note is removed. Set before first paint so
       nothing flashes. `[hidden]` is forced too: a class with its own
       `display` otherwise beats the attribute. #}
    <script>if (window.self === window.top) document.documentElement.classList.add('hy-live');</script>
    <style>.hy-live [data-hy-empty], .hy-live .hy-editor-only, .hy-live .hy-ph-hint, .hy-live .hy-ph-note { display: none !important; } [hidden] { display: none !important; }</style>
    {# The browser-chrome tint. Follows the page background, which is what the
       header paints from now that `headerColor` is derived rather than picked. #}
    <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ cs.backgroundColor | default('#0B0F0D', true) }}"/>

    {# ===== Google Fonts =====
       FOUR separate links, and a per-family weight list. Both matter.

       ── Why four links ──
       The CSS2 API returns 400 for the WHOLE request if any one family lacks a
       requested weight. On a single combined URL that means one bad weight
       silently drops all four faces and the entire storefront falls back to
       system-ui. Split, a family that cannot serve a weight fails alone.

       ── Why a per-family list ──
       This used to request one weight list for all six options in a dropdown,
       which forced the INTERSECTION: Amiri has only 400/700, so no Arabic
       display face could ever be asked for 500 or 600. Worse, the Arabic BODY
       list was 300;400;700 while the CSS asks for 500, 600 and 800 in sixteen
       places — every section title in the theme was being FAUX-bolded by the
       browser, which on Arabic thickens strokes unevenly and damages the joins.

       Only one family per slot is ever loaded, so each can be asked for exactly
       what it ships. The table below was measured against the live API, not
       guessed: anything missing from it falls back to the full range, and an
       option added to settings-schema.json must be measured and added here or
       it silently loses its heavy weights.

       `300` is deliberately absent — nothing in the theme sets it, and it was
       four extra font files per page. #}
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/>

    {# Measured against the live API — every option in settings-schema.json is
       listed. An option added there WITHOUT a row here falls back to 400;700:
       plain, but it always loads. The opposite default (the full range) would
       404 the request for any family that lacks a weight and drop that face
       entirely, which is the failure this table exists to prevent. #}
    {% set SAFE = '400;700' %}
    {% set WGHT = {
      'Amiri':                '400;700',
      'Cormorant Garamond':   '400;500;600;700',
      'Cairo':                '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'Almarai':              '400;700;800',
      'Tajawal':              '400;500;700;800',
      'Rubik':                '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'Noto Sans Arabic':     '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'IBM Plex Sans Arabic': '400;500;600;700',
      'Marhey':               '400;500;600;700',
      'El Messiri':           '400;500;600;700',
      'Figtree':              '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'Plus Jakarta Sans':    '400;500;600;700;800',
      'Manrope':              '400;500;600;700;800',
      'Poppins':              '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'Nunito':               '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'Baloo 2':              '400;500;600;700;800',
      'DM Sans':              '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'Inter':                '400;500;600;700;800;900',
      'Montserrat':           '400;500;600;700;800;900'
    } %}

    {% set wDisplayAr = WGHT[fontDisplayAr] | default(SAFE, true) %}
    {% set wDisplayEn = WGHT[fontDisplayEn] | default(SAFE, true) %}
    {% set wBodyAr    = WGHT[fontBodyAr]    | default(SAFE, true) %}
    {% set wBodyEn    = WGHT[fontBodyEn]    | default(SAFE, true) %}

    {# One family per slot, and a slot that repeats a family already requested
       is skipped. This theme's default pairing uses ONE face per script for
       both display and body (Cairo / Figtree at different weights), which under
       the old unconditional four links fetched the same stylesheet twice.

       Skipping is only safe because the family decides the weight list: two
       slots holding the same family read the same WGHT row, so the URLs are
       byte-identical and the dropped request could not have carried a weight
       the kept one lacks. Two DIFFERENT families never dedupe. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family={{ fontDisplayAr | replace(' ', '+') }}:wght@{{ wDisplayAr }}&display=swap"/>
    {% if fontDisplayEn != fontDisplayAr %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family={{ fontDisplayEn | replace(' ', '+') }}:wght@{{ wDisplayEn }}&display=swap"/>
    {% endif %}
    {% if fontBodyAr != fontDisplayAr and fontBodyAr != fontDisplayEn %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family={{ fontBodyAr | replace(' ', '+') }}:wght@{{ wBodyAr }}&display=swap"/>
    {% endif %}
    {% if fontBodyEn != fontDisplayAr and fontBodyEn != fontDisplayEn and fontBodyEn != fontBodyAr %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family={{ fontBodyEn | replace(' ', '+') }}:wght@{{ wBodyEn }}&display=swap"/>
    {% endif %}

    {% seo %}
    {% qumra_head %}

    {# After qumra_head, which prints the store's own icon: the last one wins. #}
    {% set favUrl = settings.favicon.fileUrl if (settings.favicon and settings.favicon.fileUrl) else (settings.favicon if settings.favicon is string else '') %}
    {% if favUrl %}<link rel="icon" href="{{ favUrl }}"/>{% endif %}

    {# Google Search Console — accepts the full <meta …> tag or just its content value #}
    {% set gv = (settings.googleVerification or '') | trim %}
    {% if gv %}
      {% set _gvI = gv.indexOf('content=') %}{% set _gvTail = gv.slice(_gvI + 8) | trim if _gvI > -1 else '' %}{% set gvMatch = [gv, _gvTail.slice(1).split(_gvTail.slice(0, 1))[0]] if _gvTail else false %}
      <meta name="google-site-verification" content="{{ gvMatch[1] if gvMatch else gv }}"/>
    {% endif %}


    {# ===== HYPER design system ===== #}
    {% set assetv = '5703a003' %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ 'style.css' | assets }}?v={{ assetv }}"/>
    {# ===== Theme layer =====
       assets/style.css is the Core and stays byte-identical across themes, so a
       Core fix can be copied in without merging. Everything that makes THIS
       theme look like itself (radii, card shape, type details) lives in
       assets/theme.css, loaded after it so equal-specificity rules win. The
       Core ships an empty one. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ 'theme.css' | assets }}?v={{ assetv }}"/>

    {# ===== Theme Editor overrides =====
       Colours are gated on `useCustomColors`; everything after them is not.
       See the note beside the switch above for why. ===== #}
    <style>
      :root {
      {# colours: always printed — `cs` above is empty when the switch is off #}
        {# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           NINE pickers drive THIRTY tokens. The rest are derived with
           color-mix(), which is the same call ui/section-colors.njk already
           makes for a single section, and for the reason written there:

             "A merchant who picks one brand colour should not then have to
              hand-pick its hover, its wash and its ring — and if they did,
              nothing would stop the four drifting out of relation."

           That was true per-section and it was not true here: this block used
           to expose all thirty as pickers, including three whose LABELS still
           described the previous theme ("text on blue wash", "navy hover") for
           colours this palette does not contain.

           ── Two mix targets, on purpose ──
           Tints and muted text mix toward `wellColor`, not toward white. The
           palette's neutrals are warm (#A29B93, #6E6862) and mixing ink into
           pure white lands neutral — measured 12/255 off. Mixing into the warm
           well halves that. The washes stay on `backgroundColor`, where white
           is the more faithful target.

           Ratios are measured against the original hand-picked palette, not
           guessed. Every derived value lands within 6/255 of what it replaces.
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
        {% set P  = cs.primaryColor    | default('#C9A45C', true) %}
        {% set D  = cs.dealColor       | default('#E07A5F', true) %}
        {% set BG = cs.backgroundColor | default('#0B0F0D', true) %}
        {% set W  = cs.wellColor       | default('#131A17', true) %}
        {% set T  = cs.textColor       | default('#F2ECE2', true) %}
        {% set BR = cs.borderColor     | default('#27312C', true) %}
        {% set FC = cs.footerColor     | default('#070A09', true) %}
        {% set FT = cs.footerTextColor | default('#E9E2D6', true) %}
        {% set ON_ACCENT = cs.accentTextColor | default('#14110F', true) %}
        {% set ON_DEAL   = cs.dealTextColor   | default('#14110F', true) %}

        /* Accent · ink — in this theme the action colour IS the text colour,
           so both families derive from the one brand pick. */
        --color-accent:        {{ P }};
        --color-accent-hover:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ P }} 88%, {{ BG }});
        --color-accent-dark:   color-mix(in srgb, {{ P }} 55%, #000);
        --color-accent-soft:   color-mix(in srgb, {{ P }} 40%, {{ W }});
        --color-accent-light:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ P }} 6%,  {{ BG }});
        --color-text-on-accent: {{ ON_ACCENT }};

        --color-ink:           {{ P }};
        --color-ink-hover:     color-mix(in srgb, {{ P }} 88%, {{ BG }});
        --color-ink-light:     color-mix(in srgb, {{ P }} 6%,  {{ BG }});
        --color-text-on-ink:   {{ ON_ACCENT }};

        /* Deal · the one saturated colour the theme allows itself */
        --color-deal:          {{ D }};
        --color-deal-hover:    color-mix(in srgb, {{ D }} 85%, #000);
        --color-deal-light:    color-mix(in srgb, {{ D }} 5%,  {{ BG }});
        --color-text-on-deal:  {{ ON_DEAL }};
        {% if cs.successColor %}--color-success: {{ cs.successColor }};{% endif %}
        {% if cs.warningColor %}--color-warning: {{ cs.warningColor }};{% endif %}

        /* Surfaces — cards sit ON the paper rather than above it, so the card
           surface is the page, not a second pick. */
        --color-bg-primary:    {{ BG }};
        --color-bg-secondary:  {{ BG }};
        --color-surface:       {{ BG }};
        --color-section-a:     {{ BG }};
        --color-bg-tertiary:   {{ W }};
        --color-section-b:     {{ W }};

        /* Text */
        --color-text-primary:   {{ T }};
        --color-text-secondary: color-mix(in srgb, {{ T }} 62%, {{ W }});
        /* 55%, not 40%: at 40% the labels this token paints (breadcrumb, option
           names, struck-out prices, cart notes) read 3.4–3.7:1 on the night
           ground and the well. 55% clears 4.5:1 on both. */
        --color-text-tertiary:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ T }} 55%, {{ W }});

        /* Lines */
        --color-border:        {{ BR }};
        --color-border-strong: color-mix(in srgb, {{ T }} 9%, {{ BR }});
        --color-border-hover:  {{ T }};
        --color-border-focus:  {{ P }};

        /* Header — follows the page. A merchant who wants a coloured bar has
           the header widget's own `colors_enabled`, which is where a choice
           about one section belongs. */
        --color-header:        {{ BG }};
        --color-header-text:   {{ T }};
        --color-header-border: {{ BR }};

        /* Footer — the one region with its own pair, because a dark footer
           under a light page is a real and common design, not a consequence.
           `footerTextColor` is the STRONGEST value: headings take it whole and
           everything weaker mixes toward the footer's own ground. That is what
           makes a dark footer work without a second set of pickers — mixing
           toward the background reduces contrast in whichever direction the
           footer actually goes. */
        --color-footer:            {{ FC }};
        --color-footer-heading:    {{ FT }};
        --color-footer-text:       color-mix(in srgb, {{ FT }} 72%, {{ FC }});
        --color-footer-text-muted: color-mix(in srgb, {{ FT }} 53%, {{ FC }});
        --color-footer-border:     color-mix(in srgb, {{ FT }} 11%, {{ FC }});

        /* Typography */
        --font-display-ar: '{{ fontDisplayAr }}', '{{ fontBodyAr }}', system-ui, sans-serif;
        {# The Arabic face is the SECOND slot of the Latin stacks: merchant
           content is single-language, so an English storefront still shows
           Arabic titles — with no Arabic face in the chain they fell through to
           system-ui (seen on HADIYA in English: Amiri titles became a system
           sans). A Latin font never has Arabic glyphs, so this only ever
           catches Arabic. #}
        --font-display-en: '{{ fontDisplayEn }}', '{{ fontDisplayAr }}', system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
        --font-body-ar:    '{{ fontBodyAr }}', system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
        --font-body-en:    '{{ fontBodyEn }}', '{{ fontBodyAr }}', system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
        {# ⚠️  The SECOND slot must be the ARABIC body face, and this line always
           wins — it is printed after assets/style.css, whose own declaration is
           `"Jost", "Tajawal", system-ui`. This one used to read
           `'{{ fontNum }}', '{{ fontBodyEn }}', ui-monospace` — and `fontNum`
           IS `fontBodyEn`, so the family was named twice and the Arabic face was
           dropped from the chain entirely.

           That matters because `| money` renders whatever digits the store's
           locale asks for. On Arabic-Indic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) the Latin face
           has no glyphs, so every price fell through to `ui-monospace` — a
           system monospace, in the most visible numbers in the shop. The Arabic
           body font is already loaded and carries those digits, so it is the
           correct catch. #}
        --font-num:        '{{ fontNum }}', '{{ fontBodyAr }}', system-ui, sans-serif;

        /* Shape */
        --radius-card:  {{ rCard }};
        --radius-input: {{ rInput }};
        --radius-media: {{ rMedia }};
        --radius-btn:   {{ rBtn }};

        /* Elevation — hairlines, not shadows */
        --shadow-card: none;

        /* Rhythm */
        --section-py:    {{ secPy }};
        --section-py-sm: {{ secPySm }};
        --container-max: {{ containerWidth }}px;
      }
    </style>

    {# ===== Tailwind CDN — config maps utilities onto the CSS vars ===== #}
    <script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script>
    <script>
      tailwind.config = {
        theme: {
          container: {
            center: true,
            padding: { DEFAULT: '32px', sm: '20px', lg: '32px' },
            screens: { '2xl': '1440px' },
          },
          extend: {
            colors: {
              accent: {
                DEFAULT: 'var(--color-accent)',
                hover: 'var(--color-accent-hover)',
                dark: 'var(--color-accent-dark)',
                soft: 'var(--color-accent-soft)',
                light: 'var(--color-accent-light)',
              },
              ink: {
                DEFAULT: 'var(--color-ink)',
                hover: 'var(--color-ink-hover)',
                light: 'var(--color-ink-light)',
              },
              deal: {
                DEFAULT: 'var(--color-deal)',
                hover: 'var(--color-deal-hover)',
                light: 'var(--color-deal-light)',
              },
              surface: 'var(--color-surface)',
              success: 'var(--color-success)',
              error: 'var(--color-error)',
              warning: 'var(--color-warning)',
              info: 'var(--color-info)',
              header: 'var(--color-header)',
              footer: {
                DEFAULT: 'var(--color-footer)',
                text: 'var(--color-footer-text)',
                muted: 'var(--color-footer-text-muted)',
                heading: 'var(--color-footer-heading)',
                border: 'var(--color-footer-border)',
              },
            },
            backgroundColor: {
              primary: 'var(--color-bg-primary)',
              secondary: 'var(--color-bg-secondary)',
              tertiary: 'var(--color-bg-tertiary)',
              canvas: 'var(--color-bg-primary)',
              well: 'var(--color-bg-tertiary)',
              'section-a': 'var(--color-section-a)',
              'section-b': 'var(--color-section-b)',
            },
            textColor: {
              primary: 'var(--color-text-primary)',
              secondary: 'var(--color-text-secondary)',
              tertiary: 'var(--color-text-tertiary)',
            },
            borderColor: {
              DEFAULT: 'var(--color-border)',
              soft: 'var(--color-border)',
              strong: 'var(--color-border-strong)',
              hover: 'var(--color-border-hover)',
              focus: 'var(--color-border-focus)',
            },
            fontFamily: {
              display: ['{{ fontDisplayAr }}', '{{ fontDisplayEn }}', 'system-ui', 'sans-serif'],
              'display-en': ['{{ fontDisplayEn }}', 'system-ui', 'sans-serif'],
              body: ['{{ fontBodyAr }}', 'system-ui', 'sans-serif'],
              'body-en': ['{{ fontBodyEn }}', 'system-ui', 'sans-serif'],
              {# same chain as --font-num above, and for the same reason: the
                 second slot is the ARABIC face, not a second copy of the Latin
                 one. A `font-num` utility that disagreed with the CSS variable
                 would put two different fallbacks on the same digits. #}
              num: ['{{ fontNum }}', '{{ fontBodyAr }}', 'system-ui', 'sans-serif'],
            },
            borderRadius: {
              none: '0px',
              xs: 'var(--radius-xs)',
              sm: 'var(--radius-sm)',
              DEFAULT: 'var(--radius-md)',
              md: 'var(--radius-md)',
              lg: 'var(--radius-lg)',
              xl: 'var(--radius-xl)',
              '2xl': 'var(--radius-2xl)',
              '3xl': 'var(--radius-2xl)',
              card: 'var(--radius-card)',
              btn: 'var(--radius-btn)',
              input: 'var(--radius-input)',
              media: 'var(--radius-media)',
              full: '9999px',
            },
            boxShadow: {
              xs: 'var(--shadow-xs)',
              sm: 'var(--shadow-sm)',
              DEFAULT: 'var(--shadow-sm)',
              md: 'var(--shadow-md)',
              lg: 'var(--shadow-lg)',
              xl: 'var(--shadow-xl)',
              accent: 'var(--shadow-accent)',
              deal: 'var(--shadow-deal)',
              card: 'var(--shadow-card)',
              focus: 'var(--shadow-focus)',
              none: 'none',
            },
            transitionTimingFunction: {
              out: 'cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.3, 1)',
              soft: 'cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)',
            },
            fontSize: {
              display: ['clamp(2.25rem, 5.2vw, 4rem)', { lineHeight: '1.12', letterSpacing: '0' }],
              headline: ['clamp(1.625rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem)', { lineHeight: '1.2', letterSpacing: '0' }],
            },
            spacing: {
              section: 'var(--section-py)',
              'section-sm': 'var(--section-py-sm)',
            },
          },
        },
      }
    </script>

    {# ===== Swiper.js CSS ===== #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/swiper@11.2.10/swiper-bundle.min.css"/>

    {# ===== Swiper.js =====
       MUST load in <head>, synchronously. Widgets init their sliders from an
       inline <script> that runs while the body is parsed — i.e. BEFORE the end
       of <body>. If Swiper were loaded at the bottom, `typeof Swiper` would be
       'undefined' at that moment and every slider would silently skip init. #}
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/swiper@11.2.10/swiper-bundle.min.js"></script>

    {# ===== GLightbox =====
       NOT the same as Swiper, which is why this no longer copies it.

       Swiper has to be synchronous: ten widgets init a slider from an inline
       <script> that runs while the body is still parsing, so the library must
       already exist at that moment. GLightbox has exactly ONE caller — the
       product page's gallery zoom — and it now inits on DOMContentLoaded, which
       every deferred script is guaranteed to have finished before.

       So this is `defer` and the stylesheet loads without blocking: measured
       gzipped, that is 15.3KB of script and 2.6KB of CSS off the critical path
       of the home page, every collection, the cart and the blog — pages that
       cannot open a lightbox at all. On Slow 3G those bytes alone are ~0.4s.

       The CSS trick: `media="print"` makes a stylesheet non-blocking, and onload
       swaps it back to `all`. The <noscript> covers a browser with JS off, which
       cannot run the swap — and cannot run GLightbox either, but the fallback
       costs one line and keeps the markup honest. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/glightbox@3.3.1/dist/css/glightbox.min.css"
          media="print" onload="this.media='all'; this.onload=null;"/>
    <noscript><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/glightbox@3.3.1/dist/css/glightbox.min.css"/></noscript>
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/glightbox@3.3.1/dist/js/glightbox.min.js"></script>

    {# Merchant CSS last in <head>, so it wins over the theme at equal specificity #}
    {% if settings.customCSS %}<style>{{ settings.customCSS | safe }}</style>{% endif %}
  </head>

  <body>
    {# ===== Header ===== #}
    {% template "header" %}

    {# ===== Page content ===== #}
    <main id="hy-main">{% content %}</main>

    {# ===== Footer ===== #}
    {% template "footer" %}

    {# One H1 per page. A hero with no title prints its own hidden H1 on the server;
       a template can't see that a section was removed or added twice, so those two
       cases are settled here. The store name is read from the header's logo link. #}
    <script>
      (function () {
        var all = document.querySelectorAll('h1');
        if (!all.length) {
          var brand = document.querySelector('.hy-brand');
          var name = (brand && brand.getAttribute('aria-label')) || document.title;
          var main = document.getElementById('hy-main');
          if (name && main) {
            var h = document.createElement('h1');
            h.className = 'hy-sr-only';
            h.textContent = name;
            main.insertBefore(h, main.firstChild);
          }
          return;
        }
        for (var i = 1; i < all.length; i++) {
          var h2 = document.createElement('h2');
          [].slice.call(all[i].attributes).forEach(function (a) { h2.setAttribute(a.name, a.value); });
          h2.innerHTML = all[i].innerHTML;
          all[i].parentNode.replaceChild(h2, all[i]);
        }
      })();
    </script>

    {# =====================================================================
       Quick View modal — content is fetched and injected by main.js
       (Alpine `quickView()` + #quick-view-content-dynamic are contractual)
       .taqa-overlay IS shared (cart drawer, search sheet, filters drawer) and
       stays. The panel, the close button and the padding are the quick view's
       alone, so they are .hy-qv-* and styled in "15.24 QUICK VIEW".
       The CONTENT is widgets/quick-view-product, which borrows the product
       page's own .hy-pdp-* classes so the two can never drift.
       ===================================================================== #}
    <div x-data="quickView()" @quick-view.window="openQuickView($event.detail.slug)" x-cloak>
      <div x-show="$store.modal.current === 'quick-view'"
           @click="$store.modal.close()"
           class="taqa-overlay"
           x-transition:enter="transition ease-out duration-200"
           x-transition:enter-start="opacity-0"
           x-transition:enter-end="opacity-100"
           x-transition:leave="transition ease-in duration-150"
           x-transition:leave-start="opacity-100"
           x-transition:leave-end="opacity-0"></div>

      <div x-show="$store.modal.current === 'quick-view'"
           x-transition:enter="transition ease-out duration-200"
           x-transition:enter-start="opacity-0 translate-y-3 sm:translate-y-0 sm:scale-[0.98]"
           x-transition:enter-end="opacity-100 translate-y-0 sm:scale-100"
           x-transition:leave="transition ease-in duration-150"
           x-transition:leave-start="opacity-100"
           x-transition:leave-end="opacity-0"
           class="hy-qv-modal">
        <div class="hy-qv-panel" @click.outside="$store.modal.close()">
          {# Not .hy-icon-btn: that one is built for the header row — it inherits
             `--color-header-text` and carries a -5px optical nudge, both wrong
             on a white panel. And not Tailwind's `w-4 h-4` on the icon: the Play
             CDN generates its classes only after its own script runs, so an
             AJAX-injected modal painted its X at the SVG's intrinsic size first. #}
          <button @click="$store.modal.close()"
                  type="button"
                  aria-label="{{ t('common.close') }}"
                  class="hy-qv-close">
            <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                 stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" aria-hidden="true">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" d="M6 18 18 6M6 6l12 12"/>
            </svg>
          </button>

          {# .hy-qv-pad, not Tailwind's p-6: the Play CDN only generates its
             classes after its own script runs, so a CDN-only padding leaves the
             first paint of an AJAX-injected modal flush against the panel edge. #}
          <div class="hy-qv-pad">
            {# Loading #}
            <div x-show="loading" class="hy-qv-loading">
              <span class="hy-spinner" style="width: 28px; height: 28px;"></span>
            </div>

            {# Error #}
            <div x-show="error && !loading" class="hy-empty">
              <span>
                <svg class="w-8 h-8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                  <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <p class="m-0 text-sm">{{t('common.error')}}</p>
            </div>

            {# Injected product markup — Alpine.initTree() runs on it #}
            <div x-show="!loading && !error" id="quick-view-content-dynamic"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    {# ===== Toast host (id is contractual with main.js) ===== #}
    {# Positioned and styled in assets/style.css → "12. FEEDBACK · TOAST".
       The utility classes that used to live here pinned it at top: 20px, which
       is across the sticky header — and they came from the Play CDN, so they
       did not exist for the first frames of a load. The id is the contract with
       Toast._show() in main.js; nothing else about this element is. #}
    <div id="toast-container"></div>

    {# ===== Alpine.js =====
       Stays at the bottom and deferred: main.js registers the stores and
       components on `alpine:init`, so Alpine must boot after main.js is parsed.
       Swiper/GLightbox are in <head> instead — see the note there.
       Alpine plugins MUST be deferred before the Alpine core script. #}
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@alpinejs/collapse@3.17.5/dist/cdn.min.js"></script>
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/alpinejs@3.17.5/dist/cdn.min.js"></script>

    {# ===== Runtime config consumed by main.js ===== #}
    <script>
      window.__QUMRA_CONFIG__ = {
        currency: '{{ localization.currency.currencyCode | default("EGP", true) }}',
        currencySymbol: '{{ localization.currency.currencySymbol | default("ج.م", true) }}',
        language: '{{ lang }}',
        exchangeRate: {{ localization.market.exchangeRate | default(1, true) }},
        {# probe for main.js `_moneyFormat` — 1234567 is wide enough that the
           result shows the thousands separator AND the decimals in one go.
           Keep this number in sync with MONEY_PROBE in assets/main.js. #}
        moneyFormatSample: '{{ 1234567 | money }}',
        cartTotalQuantity: {{ cart.totalQuantity | default(0, true) }},
        {# Read by CartManager._refreshShipBar() on DOMContentLoaded, so the
           free-shipping bar can be recomputed with the DETECTED minor-unit
           scale before the drawer is ever opened. In minor units, like every
           other price that crosses this boundary. #}
        cartTotalPrice: {{ cart.totalPrice | default(0, true) }},
        wishlistIds: [{% for p in wishlist.products %}'{{ p._id }}'{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}{% endfor %}],
        messages: {
          addedToCart: '{{t("pages.product.added_to_cart")}}',
          addError: '{{t("common.error")}}',
          outOfStock: '{{t("cart.out_of_stock")}}'
        },
        {# Single source of truth for HayatCard.build() — widgets must NOT
           dump their own copies of these strings. #}
        card: {
          addToCart: {{ t('pages.product.add_to_cart') | dump | safe }},
          added: {{ t('pages.product.added') | dump | safe }},
          chooseOptions: {{ t('pages.product.choose_options') | dump | safe }},
          viewProduct: {{ t('pages.product.view_product') | dump | safe }},
          outOfStock: {{ t('pages.product.out_of_stock') | dump | safe }},
          quickView: {{ t('pages.product.quick_view') | dump | safe }},
          onSale: {{ t('common.on_sale') | dump | safe }},
          {# Read by Utils.formatPrice() — a price of 0 reads as a word, not
             as "0.00". Only product PRICES use it; totals keep formatMoney. #}
          free: {{ t('common.free') | dump | safe }},
          noProducts: {{ t('products.no_products') | dump | safe }},
          noResults: {{ t('pages.search.no_results') | dump | safe }},
          isNew: {{ t('products.new') | dump | safe }},
          isPopular: {{ t('products.popular') | dump | safe }},
          isHot: {{ t('products.hot') | dump | safe }},
          sellingFast: {{ t('products.selling_fast') | dump | safe }},
          tryDifferent: {{ t('pages.search.try_different') | dump | safe }},
          {# Read by collectionFilter.applyPrice() when it corrects a typed
             bound — see the note there for why it corrects rather than refuses. #}
          priceNegative: {{ t('pages.search.price_negative') | dump | safe }},
          priceSwapped: {{ t('pages.search.price_swapped') | dump | safe }},
          clearAll: {{ t('pages.search.clear_all') | dump | safe }},
          products: {{ t('pages.search.products') | dump | safe }},
          resultsFor: {{ t('pages.search.results_for') | dump | safe }},
          home: {{ t('common.home') | dump | safe }},
          prev: {{ t('common.prev') | dump | safe }},
          next: {{ t('common.next') | dump | safe }}
        },
        {# Utils.count() in main.js, twin of ui/count.njk. #}
        counts: {
          one: {{ t('counts.product_one') | dump | safe }},
          two: {{ t('counts.product_two') | dump | safe }},
          few: {{ t('counts.product_few') | dump | safe }},
          many: {{ t('counts.product_many') | dump | safe }}
        },
        {# Strings for the JS cart-line renderer — same single-source rule. #}
        cart: {
          remove: {{ t('pages.cart.remove_item') | dump | safe }},
          removeConfirm: {{ t('pages.cart.remove_confirm') | dump | safe }},
          unitPrice: {{ t('pages.cart.unit_price') | dump | safe }},
          {# `{amount}` is substituted by _refreshShipBar(); the token has to
             match the one in locales/*.json or the message renders with the
             placeholder still in it. #}
          freeShipLeft: {{ t('pages.cart.free_shipping_left') | dump | safe }},
          freeShipDone: {{ t('pages.cart.free_shipping_done') | dump | safe }},
          deleteLabel: {{ t('common.delete') | dump | safe }},
          cancel: {{ t('common.cancel') | dump | safe }}
        },
        {# Product-card defaults for the AJAX renderer, which has to match the
           server-rendered partial or a filtered grid would drop the New badge
           and the low-stock strip that the grid above it shows.

           ⚠️  These were Theme Editor settings. They are now literals, and
           ui/product-card.njk falls back to THE SAME values — the two lists
           must be changed together or the SSR card and the AJAX card drift.
           A section that wants different values passes them per widget. #}
        cardDefaults: {
          imageRatio: '2x3',
          showSecondImage: true,
          showQuickView: true,
          showBadge: true,
          badgeStyle: 'vertical',
          atcStyle: 'tray',
          showNewBadge: true,
          newTag: 'new',
          popularTag: 'popular',
          hotTag: 'hot',
          lowStock: {{ (settings.lowStockThreshold if (settings.lowStockThreshold or settings.lowStockThreshold == 0) else 5) | dump | safe }}
        }
      };
    </script>

    {% qumra_scripts %}

    {# main.js registers the Alpine stores/components — must run before Alpine boots #}
    <script src="{{ 'main.js' | assets }}?v={{ assetv }}"></script>
    {% if settings.customJS %}<script>{{ settings.customJS | safe }}</script>{% endif %}
  </body>
</html>
